/* FMT 4: Thanatology */
Z("fmt4",0,"f",["Somatic (systemic/clinical) death is the complete and irreversible stoppage of circulation, respiration and brain function (the person as a whole dies), while molecular (cellular) death is the death of individual cells and tissues that follows over the next few hours at different rates; brain death is irreversible loss of all functions of the brain including the brainstem, legally recognised as death in India under THOTA 1994 for organ donation.","The person dies first (somatic), the cells die later one by one (molecular) — that gap is why organs can be transplanted and muscles still twitch after death.",["Somatic death: cessation of the three vital functions (Bichat's tripod — heart, lungs, brain); diagnosed clinically; molecular death follows ~3–4 h later (nervous tissue in 5 min, muscles 1–2 h, cornea 6 h, skin/bones 12–24 h).","Brain death: irreversible coma, absent brainstem reflexes (pupillary, corneal, oculocephalic, oculovestibular/caloric, gag, cough), apnoea test (no respiratory effort at PaCO₂ ≥ 60 mmHg), known irreversible cause; excluded — hypothermia, drugs, metabolic, shock.","India — THOTA 1994 (amended 2011; Rules 2014): Board of four doctors — registered medical practitioner in charge of hospital, an independent specialist, a neurologist/neurosurgeon, the treating doctor; tests repeated after ≥ 6 h; certification in Form 10.","Suspended animation (apparent death): vital functions at minimal level, undetectable; seen in drowning, electrocution, hypothermia, newborns, cholera, yoga; resuscitation possible — hence caution before certifying death."],"Brainstem death = death (THOTA); 4-member board, tests repeated after 6 h."],
"Death is defined as the permanent cessation of life. Types: (1) somatic (systemic, clinical) death — complete, persistent and irreversible cessation of the vital functions of the brain, heart and lungs, which maintain life and health (the 'tripod of life' — Bichat); the individual ceases to be a sentient, integrated organism; signs — insensibility, loss of movement, cessation of circulation and respiration; it is diagnosed by the doctor at the bedside and its moment is the legal time of death; (2) molecular (cellular) death — death of individual cells and tissues, which occurs piecemeal after somatic death because tissues differ in their tolerance of anoxia; it is usually complete about 3–4 hours (up to 24 h for some tissues) after somatic death; neurons die within 3–7 minutes of anoxia, cardiac muscle in ~15–30 min, skeletal muscles survive 1–3 hours (respond to electrical or mechanical stimulation — idiomuscular contraction; supravital reactions), kidney ~45 min, liver ~1 h, cornea ~6 h, skin, tendons and bones up to 12–24 hours, white blood cells ~12 h, spermatozoa remain motile in the body for several hours; this interval permits harvesting of organs and tissues for transplantation (cornea within 6 h, skin within 6 h, bone, heart valves) and explains supravital reactions used in estimating time since death. Brain death (brainstem death): irreversible cessation of all functions of the entire brain including the brainstem, even though the heart may continue to beat and circulation and oxygenation are maintained artificially by a ventilator; types — cortical (cerebral) death with intact brainstem results in persistent vegetative state (PVS — patient breathes spontaneously, has sleep–wake cycles but no awareness) — not legal death; brainstem death — loss of brainstem functions (respiration, consciousness, reflexes) — legal death; whole brain death (USA). Legal status in India: the Transplantation of Human Organs Act, 1994 (THOA; amended 2011 as THOTA — Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act) recognises 'brain-stem death' — 'the stage at which all functions of the brain-stem have permanently and irreversibly ceased' — as death; certification by a Board of medical experts consisting of (a) the registered medical practitioner in charge of the hospital where brainstem death occurred, (b) an independent registered medical practitioner (specialist) nominated from a panel approved by the appropriate authority, (c) a neurologist or neurosurgeon (2014 Rules — if not available, a surgeon/physician/anaesthetist/intensivist nominated), and (d) the registered medical practitioner treating the patient; none of them should be part of the transplant team; tests are performed twice with an interval of at least 6 hours (adults) and certified in Form 10. Criteria for diagnosis of brainstem death: (A) preconditions — patient in deep apnoeic coma on a ventilator; irreversible structural brain damage due to a known cause (e.g., head injury, intracranial haemorrhage, hypoxic encephalopathy) — confirmed by imaging; (B) exclusions (reversible causes must be excluded) — depressant drugs (sedatives, opioids, alcohol), neuromuscular blocking agents, hypothermia (core temperature < 32–35 °C), severe metabolic or endocrine disturbances (hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, uraemia, hepatic encephalopathy, hypothyroidism), shock/hypotension; (C) clinical tests — absence of brainstem reflexes: (1) pupils fixed and unresponsive to bright light (direct and consensual), may be mid-size or dilated; (2) absent corneal reflex; (3) absent oculocephalic reflex (doll's eye movements — eyes move with head on rotation); (4) absent oculovestibular reflex (cold caloric test — 50 ml ice-cold water in each ear with head at 30° — no eye movement); (5) no motor response within the cranial nerve distribution to painful stimulation (supraorbital pressure); no grimacing; (6) absent gag reflex (stimulation of pharynx) and cough reflex (suction catheter in trachea/bronchi); (7) apnoea test — after pre-oxygenation with 100% oxygen, the patient is disconnected from the ventilator with oxygen insufflation via tracheal catheter; absence of spontaneous respiratory effort despite rise of PaCO₂ to ≥ 60 mmHg (or 20 mmHg above baseline) and pH < 7.28 confirms apnoea; spinal reflexes may persist (do not exclude brain death); (D) confirmatory/ancillary tests (not mandatory in India; used when clinical tests cannot be completed) — EEG (isoelectric — electrocerebral silence), cerebral angiography (absent intracranial flow), transcranial Doppler, radionuclide scan ('hollow skull'), CT angiography. Importance of brain death: organ donation (heart, lungs, liver, kidneys, pancreas, intestine) from deceased donors (cadaveric donation) — consent of next of kin, NOTTO/ROTTO/SOTTO network; withdrawal of futile life support; medicolegal — if brain death is due to an injury (homicide), the person who caused it is liable for death; time of death legally is the time of the second set of tests. Suspended animation (apparent death): a condition in which the vital functions (respiration, circulation) are at such a low level that they cannot be detected by routine clinical methods, but life persists and resuscitation is possible; may last from seconds to an hour (rarely longer); voluntary — yogis; involuntary — newborn infants, drowning, electrocution, lightning, hypothermia (cold exposure — 'nobody is dead until warm and dead'), cholera, sunstroke, shock, narcotic poisoning (barbiturates, opioids), cerebral concussion, hysteria; importance — death should not be certified hastily in such cases; prolonged resuscitation and confirmation of irreversible signs (rigor, lividity) required before certification; risk of premature burial/autopsy.",
[["Somatic vs molecular death",[["Feature","Somatic death","Molecular death"],["Definition","Death of person as a whole (brain, heart, lungs)","Death of individual cells/tissues"],["Timing","At the moment of death","Follows over hours (3–4 h to 24 h)"],["Diagnosis","Clinical signs","Loss of supravital reactions"],["Significance","Legal time of death","Organ/tissue donation; supravital reactions"]]],
["Survival of tissues after somatic death",[["Tissue","Approximate survival"],["Neurons","3–7 min"],["Heart","15–30 min"],["Kidney","~45 min"],["Skeletal muscle","1–3 h"],["Cornea","~6 h"],["Skin, bone, tendon","12–24 h"]]],
["Brainstem death criteria",[["Step","Details"],["Preconditions","Apnoeic coma on ventilator, known irreversible cause"],["Exclusions","Drugs, hypothermia, metabolic/endocrine, shock"],["Absent reflexes","Pupillary, corneal, oculocephalic, caloric, motor to pain in CN area, gag, cough"],["Apnoea test","No breathing at PaCO₂ ≥ 60 mmHg"],["Repeat","After ≥ 6 h by 4-member board (THOTA)"]]],
["Suspended animation",["Drowning, electrocution, hypothermia","Newborns, cholera, narcotic poisoning","Yoga (voluntary)","Resuscitate; do not certify hastily"]]],
{c:"Somatic death is the irreversible cessation of brain, heart and lung function of the individual, followed hours later by molecular death of cells; brainstem death, diagnosed by a four-member board through absent brainstem reflexes and a positive apnoea test repeated after six hours, is legal death under THOTA and enables organ donation, while suspended animation must be excluded before certification."});

Z("fmt4",1,"f",["Signs of death are classified as immediate (cessation of nervous, circulatory and respiratory functions — somatic death), early (cooling of body, changes in eyes and skin, post-mortem lividity, rigor mortis — within hours) and late (decomposition and its modifications — adipocere, mummification); they confirm death and help estimate the time since death.","Immediate signs tell you death has happened, early signs tell you it happened hours ago, late signs tell you days or more.",["Immediate: insensibility, loss of reflexes, flat EEG; cessation of heartbeat and pulse (auscultation ≥ 5 min), ECG flat; cessation of respiration (Winslow's test, mirror test, feather test — historical); pallor and loss of elasticity of skin, primary flaccidity of muscles.","Early: changes in eyes — loss of corneal reflex, corneal clouding (within 2 h if eyes open, 12–24 h closed), tache noire (brownish-black patches on sclera — 3–4 h when eyes open), flaccid eyeball/fall of IOP, segmentation of retinal vessels ('trucking'/'cattle-trucking' — within minutes), pupils mid-dilated; algor mortis (cooling); livor mortis (PM lividity — 1–2 h); rigor mortis (2–3 h).","Late: putrefaction (green discolouration RIF 12–18 h, marbling 36–48 h, bloating, maggots, skeletonisation); modifications — adipocere, mummification.","Tests of historical interest: Magnus's ligature test (finger ligature — no congestion after death), Icard's test (fluorescein injection), diaphanous test (no red glow through fingers), Winslow's (water bowl on chest); ECG/EEG modern confirmation."],"Cattle-trucking of retinal vessels — earliest eye sign; tache noire — open eyes."],
"The diagnosis of death is based on signs that appear in a sequence; they are classified as: (I) immediate signs (signs of somatic death) — (1) cessation of nervous system functions: complete and continuous insensibility to all stimuli, loss of voluntary movements and sensation, absence of all reflexes (corneal, pupillary, deep tendon); flat (isoelectric) EEG; muscles become flaccid (primary relaxation); (2) cessation of circulation: absence of pulse (carotid, radial, femoral) and heart sounds on auscultation for at least 5 minutes (a minimum of one minute at each of the cardiac areas — total 5 min recommended), flat ECG (asystole) for 5 min; tests of historical interest — Magnus's test (a ligature tied tightly around the base of a finger produces bluish-white congestion distally in life but no change after death), Icard's test (subcutaneous injection of fluorescein turns skin yellow-green in life, not after death), diaphanous test (fingers held against a strong light show red glow in life due to circulation — absent in death), opening a small artery (no spurting), heating the skin (a blister containing fluid forms in life; in death — blister contains vapour/air), finger-nail pressure test (blanching and refilling absent), ophthalmoscopy (retinal changes — see below); (3) cessation of respiration: absence of respiratory movements and breath sounds on auscultation for 5 minutes; historical tests — feather test (feather before nostrils doesn't move), mirror test (cold mirror before mouth and nose doesn't fog), Winslow's test (bowl of water or mercury placed on the chest shows no ripples); (4) changes in skin: pale, ashen and loses elasticity; lips may turn brown and hard due to drying. (II) Early signs (changes in the first few hours to about 24–48 h; signs of molecular death) — (1) changes in the eyes: loss of corneal reflex (immediately); pupils mid-dilated, fixed (become irregular later; pupils may react to drugs — supravital reaction — atropine/pilocarpine for ~ 4 h); segmentation of the blood columns in retinal vessels ('trucking' or 'cattle-trucking' or 'box-carring') — seen on ophthalmoscopy within minutes of death — earliest and reliable sign; retina pale; loss of intraocular tension (eyeball becomes soft and flaccid; IOP falls < 12 mmHg in 15 min and nil by 2 h); clouding (haziness, opacity) of the cornea due to drying and loss of turgor (within 10 min–2 h if eyelids are open; ~ 6–12 h, later if closed); tache noire de la sclérotique (Sommer and Larcher's sign) — triangular yellowish then brownish-black discolouration of the exposed sclera on both sides of the cornea due to drying, when eyes remain partly open, within 3–6 hours; fall of potassium concentration rises in vitreous humour (used for time since death); (2) cooling of the body (algor mortis); (3) post-mortem lividity (livor mortis, hypostasis) — appears 30 min–2 h, fixed 6–8 h; (4) muscular changes — primary relaxation/flaccidity (1–2 h), rigor mortis (begins 1–2 h, complete 12 h, lasts 12 h, passes off 24–36 h in India's climate), secondary relaxation; cadaveric spasm (instantaneous rigor in special circumstances); (5) other early changes — skin pallor, loss of elasticity, drying of lips, scrotum and wounds (parchmentisation), dehydration, post-mortem clotting of blood. (III) Late signs (beyond 24 h) — (1) decomposition (putrefaction) — autolysis and bacterial action: greenish discolouration of the right iliac fossa (12–18 h in summer in India), marbling of skin (veins appear dark green-purple, 36–48 h), gaseous distension (bloating) of face, abdomen, scrotum/penis, protrusion of eyes and tongue, purging of blood-stained fluid from mouth and nose, skin slippage and blisters, degloving of hands and feet, loosening of hair and nails, maggots (fly eggs within hours; maggots in 24 h), colliquative liquefaction, skeletonisation (weeks–months); (2) modifications of decomposition — adipocere formation (saponification, in moist warm conditions — weeks), mummification (desiccation in dry, hot, airy conditions — weeks to months); (3) skeletonisation. Importance: confirmation of death (avoiding premature certification — exclude suspended animation), estimation of time since death, determination of position of body after death and whether it was moved (lividity), cause of death indicators (colour of lividity), and differentiation of antemortem from postmortem changes.",
[["Classification of signs of death",[["Category","Signs","Time"],["Immediate","Insensibility, loss of reflexes, flat EEG; absent pulse/heart sounds (5 min), flat ECG; no respiration; pallor, primary flaccidity","At death"],["Early","Eye changes, algor mortis, livor mortis, rigor mortis","Minutes to 24–48 h"],["Late","Putrefaction, adipocere, mummification, skeletonisation","> 24 h"]]],
["Eye changes after death",[["Change","Timing"],["Loss of corneal and pupillary reflexes","Immediate"],["Segmentation of retinal vessels (cattle-trucking)","Within minutes"],["Fall of intraocular tension","15 min–2 h"],["Corneal clouding","~2 h (open) / 6–12 h (closed)"],["Tache noire","3–6 h (eyes open)"],["Vitreous potassium rise","Linear with time"]]],
["Historical tests",["Magnus's ligature test","Icard's fluorescein test","Diaphanous test","Winslow's, mirror, feather tests","Heating skin: vapour blister"]]],
{c:"Death is diagnosed by immediate signs of cessation of nervous, circulatory and respiratory functions, followed by early changes in eyes, cooling, lividity and rigor within hours, and late changes of decomposition, adipocere and mummification, which together confirm death and estimate the post-mortem interval."});

Z("fmt4",2,"f",["Post-mortem lividity (livor mortis, hypostasis, suggillation) is the bluish-purple discolouration of the dependent parts of the body due to gravitational pooling of blood in the capillaries and venules after circulation stops; it appears in 30 min–2 h, is well developed by 4–6 h and becomes fixed in 6–8 h (in India), sparing areas of pressure (contact pallor), and its colour may indicate the cause of death (cherry-red in CO, brown in nitrites, pink in cyanide/cold).","When the heart stops, blood sinks like sediment to the lowest parts — the stain shows how the body lay and, once fixed, whether it was moved.",["Mechanism: gravity → blood settles in dependent vessels; fixation due to haemolysis and diffusion of pigment into tissues, and capillary wall damage, plus rigor of vessel walls.","Timing (India): appears 30 min–1 h as patches, confluent 3–6 h, fixed 6–8 h (blanches on pressure before fixation; shifts if body turned before fixation); persists till putrefaction.","Distribution: supine — back, buttocks, posterior limbs, sparing shoulder blades, buttocks and calves (contact pallor); hanging — lower limbs, hands, genitals (glove-and-stocking); drowning — face, upper chest, head (head-down); Tardieu spots — petechiae in lividity.","Colour: normal bluish purple; cherry-red — CO poisoning (carboxyHb); bright pink — cyanide, hypothermia, refrigeration; chocolate/coffee brown — nitrites, chlorates, aniline (methHb); bronze — Clostridium perfringens septic abortion; deep blue — asphyxia; pale — anaemia, haemorrhage.","Lividity vs bruise: PM vs AM; dependent parts vs anywhere; no swelling vs swelling; blood within vessels (incision — blood oozes, washes away) vs extravasated (doesn't wash off); no colour change vs colour changes; edges well-defined."],"Contact pallor; fixed at 6–8 h; cherry-red CO, brown nitrites."],
"Post-mortem lividity (synonyms — livor mortis, hypostasis, post-mortem staining, suggillation, vibices, cadaveric lividity) is the bluish-purple or purplish-red discolouration of the skin and organs in the dependent parts of the body after death, caused by the accumulation of blood under the influence of gravity in the dilated capillaries and small veins after the circulation ceases. Mechanism: after death, the blood loses its propelling force and settles by gravity into the lowest (dependent) parts; the blood remains fluid for some hours (fibrinolysins in sudden deaths); the vessels in the dependent parts become distended, producing the colour; initially the blood is within the vessels and can be displaced by pressure (blanching) or by changing the position of the body (redistribution — 'shifting'); later, due to haemoconcentration (loss of fluid through vessel walls), haemolysis with diffusion of haemoglobin into surrounding tissues, clotting, and rigor mortis of the muscular walls of vessels, the staining becomes 'fixed' and does not blanch or shift. Timing (in Indian climate; variable): begins as small, irregular, dull red patches within 30 minutes to 1–2 hours after death (may be apparent before death in moribund persons with failing circulation); the patches coalesce to form large areas by 3–6 hours; well developed and maximal at 6–12 hours; fixation occurs in about 6–8 hours (complete fixation by 12 hours; in cold climates later); after fixation, turning the body produces a secondary lividity in the newly dependent areas while the primary lividity persists (double lividity indicates the body was moved after some hours); lividity persists until decomposition, when it becomes greenish due to sulphhaemoglobin formation. Distribution: depends on the position of the body after death: (1) supine position (most common) — back of the neck, trunk, buttocks, posterior aspects of limbs; areas in contact with the hard surface (shoulder blades, buttocks, back of calves, heels) remain pale because capillaries are compressed (contact pallor/contact flattening — pressure pallor); tight clothing, belts, collar, brassiere, socks produce pale bands (may mimic ligature marks); folds of skin; (2) prone position — front of the body, face (may simulate bruising — face congestion), pale areas on forehead, nose, chin, chest; (3) hanging (body suspended for hours) — lower limbs, feet, hands, forearms, external genitalia, lower abdomen ('glove and stocking' distribution); (4) drowning — body floats with head down and limbs dependent; lividity in face, upper chest, head and hands (but movement in water may make it ill-defined); (5) lateral position — lower side; (6) internal organs also show hypostasis — posterior parts of lungs (mimicking pneumonia), dependent loops of intestine (mimicking strangulation/infarction), posterior myocardium, brain, kidney; (7) small petechial haemorrhages (Tardieu spots) may develop within areas of intense lividity due to rupture of distended capillaries (after 18–24 h; not necessarily asphyxial); (8) lividity may be absent or faint in severe anaemia, haemorrhage, cachexia, in bodies kept in water with constant movement, and when the body is moved frequently. Colour of lividity (medicolegal importance): normally bluish-purple or reddish-purple (due to reduced haemoglobin); cherry-red (bright pink-red) — carbon monoxide poisoning (carboxyhaemoglobin); bright red/pink — cyanide poisoning (oxygenated blood as tissues cannot utilise O₂; cyanmethaemoglobin), hypothermia, refrigeration/cold storage (oxygen retained by cold), and in bodies immersed in cold water (drowning); chocolate brown/coffee brown/slate grey — methaemoglobinaemia in poisoning by nitrites, nitrates, chlorates, aniline, nitrobenzene, potassium chlorate, dapsone, phenacetin; bronze/copper brown — Clostridium perfringens (welchii) septicaemia (septic abortion); deep blue/purple-violet — asphyxial deaths (congestion, venous stasis); dark brown/yellow — phosphorus poisoning (jaundice); greenish — hydrogen sulphide poisoning (sulphhaemoglobin), putrefaction. Medicolegal importance: (1) sign of death; (2) helps estimate the time since death (approximately); (3) indicates the position of the body at the time of death and in the first hours (and change of position — moving of body after death if lividity in non-dependent parts or double lividity — suggests foul play/staging); (4) colour may indicate the cause of death (CO, cyanide, nitrites, hypothermia); (5) pressure pallor patterns may reveal objects on which the body lay or tight clothing; (6) lividity must be distinguished from bruises (contusions) — an important cause of error in autopsy and allegation of assault. Differences between post-mortem lividity and bruise: lividity — produced after death; situated in dependent parts only; no swelling or elevation of skin; margins well defined, uniform colour; there is no damage to epithelium; on incision blood is seen within vessels (oozing from cut vessels as small dots) and is easily washed off by water leaving the tissue pale; no colour changes over days (except putrefactive green); not associated with injury; disappears/shifts on pressure or change of position in early stages; microscopically — no extravasation, no inflammatory reaction; bruise (antemortem) — produced during life by trauma; can occur anywhere (often over bony prominences); usually swollen and elevated due to effusion; margins ill-defined, irregular; may be associated with abrasion/injury of skin; on incision — extravasated, clotted blood infiltrating the tissues, not washed away by water; colour changes with time (red → blue → green → yellow → normal); does not shift with position; microscopically — extravasated RBCs with inflammatory reaction, haemosiderin later; enzyme histochemistry. Differentiation of hypostasis in organs from congestion due to disease (e.g., lungs — pneumonia; intestines — strangulation; heart — infarct) is also important.",
[["Time course (India)",[["Stage","Time"],["Patches appear","30 min–2 h"],["Confluent","3–6 h"],["Fixed","6–8 h (complete by 12 h)"],["Persists","Till putrefaction (turns green)"]]],
["Distribution by position",[["Position","Lividity site"],["Supine","Back, buttocks, posterior limbs; contact pallor over scapulae, buttocks, calves"],["Prone","Face, chest, front"],["Hanging","Lower limbs, hands, genitalia (glove and stocking)"],["Drowning","Face, head, upper chest"]]],
["Colour and cause",[["Colour","Cause"],["Bluish purple","Normal"],["Cherry red","Carbon monoxide"],["Bright pink/red","Cyanide, hypothermia, refrigeration"],["Chocolate/coffee brown","Nitrites, chlorates, aniline (methaemoglobin)"],["Bronze","Clostridium perfringens (septic abortion)"],["Deep blue","Asphyxia"],["Yellow-brown","Phosphorus (jaundice)"]]],
["Lividity vs bruise",[["Feature","PM lividity","Bruise"],["Time","After death","Antemortem"],["Site","Dependent parts","Anywhere"],["Swelling","Absent","Present"],["Margins","Well defined","Ill defined"],["On incision","Blood in vessels, washes off","Extravasated, doesn't wash off"],["Colour changes","No","Yes"],["Microscopy","No inflammation","Inflammatory reaction"]]]],
{c:"Post-mortem lividity is gravitational pooling of blood in dependent parts appearing within 2 hours and fixing at 6–8 hours, with contact pallor; its distribution indicates posture and movement of the body, its colour may point to CO, cyanide or nitrite poisoning, and it must be distinguished from bruises."});

Z("fmt4",3,"f",["Rigor mortis is the stiffening of all muscles after death due to depletion of ATP and formation of fixed actin–myosin cross-links; in India it begins 1–2 h after death in the myocardium and then small muscles (eyelids → face → neck → upper limbs → trunk → lower limbs — Nysten's rule), is complete in 12 h, persists ~12 h and passes off in the same order by 24–36 h; cadaveric spasm (instantaneous rigor) is the stiffening of a group of muscles at the moment of death without primary relaxation, seen in violent deaths with intense emotion (e.g., a weapon gripped firmly in suicide, grass/weeds clutched in drowning) and cannot be produced after death.","Rigor is the slow 'locking' of all muscles once the body's energy runs out; cadaveric spasm is a frozen last action captured at the instant of death — the hand still clenching what it held.",["Mechanism: ATP depletion → actin–myosin cross-bridges fixed (rigor complex); lactic acid; passes off due to autolysis/proteolysis of myofibrils (putrefaction).","Order (Nysten's rule): involuntary muscles (heart — 1 h) first; voluntary — eyelids, face, jaw, neck, upper limbs, trunk, lower limbs, fingers/toes last; disappears in same order; India: onset 1–2 h, full 12 h, lasts 12 h, passes 24–36 h (summer 18–36 h; winter 24–48 h).","Factors: early onset and short duration — high temperature, exhaustion, convulsions (strychnine, tetanus), electrocution, violent struggle, emaciation, infants/old; delayed and prolonged — cold, healthy muscular adults, asphyxia, sudden death; absent in fetus < 7 months.","Conditions simulating rigor: heat stiffening (> 65 °C — burns, pugilistic attitude), cold stiffening (freezing of fat and fluids — crackles; passes on warming), gas stiffening (putrefaction).","Cadaveric spasm vs rigor: at death vs 1–2 h after; no primary relaxation vs after relaxation; localised group (hand) vs all muscles; mechanism — uncertain, nervous vs ATP depletion; needs great force to break vs broken easily (doesn't return); cannot be simulated after death; medicolegal — indicates manner of death (suicide, drowning, struggle) vs time since death."],"Rigor order: eyelids → face → neck → arms → trunk → legs (Nysten). Spasm = no primary flaccidity."],
"Muscle changes after death occur in three stages: (1) primary relaxation (primary flaccidity) — immediately after death all muscles relax (somatic death) and the body becomes flaccid; the muscles still respond to mechanical and electrical stimuli (molecular life, idiomuscular contraction) and pupils react to drugs; the jaw falls, eyelids lose tension, joints are flexible; this stage lasts about 1–2 hours (3–6 h in some); contact flattening occurs in this stage; (2) rigor mortis (cadaveric rigidity, death stiffening) — stiffening and slight shortening of muscles, which become firm, opaque and do not respond to stimuli; (3) secondary relaxation (secondary flaccidity) — muscles become soft again due to the onset of decomposition (autolysis and putrefaction). Mechanism of rigor mortis: in life, ATP binds to myosin heads, allowing detachment from actin (relaxation) and providing energy for contraction; ATP is resynthesised from glycogen (aerobic and anaerobic glycolysis) and creatine phosphate; after death, ATP resynthesis ceases; as ATP levels fall below ~85% of normal, cross-bridges between actin and myosin cannot be released and they form a stable actomyosin complex (rigor complex); when ATP falls to ~15%, rigidity is complete; glycolysis produces lactic acid (pH falls from ~7.2 to 5.8); rigor passes off when the actomyosin complex is broken down by proteolytic enzymes (cathepsins, calpains) released during autolysis, and by bacterial putrefaction — not by restoration of ATP. Rigor appears in all muscles (voluntary and involuntary) at the same time but becomes apparent first in smaller muscles. Order of appearance (Nysten's rule, 1811 — though muscles stiffen simultaneously, it is first detectable in smaller muscles): involuntary muscles — myocardium (within 1 h — causes left ventricle to contract and may mimic hypertrophy; 'stone heart' in heat), then voluntary muscles in a descending order — eyelids (orbicularis oculi), muscles of the face (lower jaw — masseters), neck, upper limbs (shoulders, arms, forearms), chest and trunk (back, abdomen), lower limbs (thighs, legs), and lastly the small muscles of fingers and toes; it passes off in the same order in which it appeared (eyelids first, lower limbs last); also seen in smooth muscle — erector pili muscles (cutis anserina — goose skin, 'gooseflesh'), iris (pupils may become irregular and unequal), dartos (shrinking of scrotum), seminal vesicles (post-mortem discharge of semen), uterus (rarely expulsion of fetus — coffin birth with putrefaction gas), intestines. Time course: highly variable; in temperate countries — onset 3–6 h, fully developed in 12 h, persists 12 h, passes off in further 12 h ('rule of 12s'); in India (tropical climate) — begins in 1–2 hours, fully established in 12 hours (head to foot in ~ 12 h), maintained for about 12 hours, passes off in 12 hours — so the body is fully rigid between 12 and 24 h and it disappears by 24–36 h in summer (18–36 h) and may last 24–48 h in winter (up to 72 h or more in very cold climates). Testing: by trying to flex or extend the joints (jaw, elbow, knee, fingers) — once broken forcibly (e.g., to dress the body), rigor does not reappear (unless broken very early — partial re-establishment). Factors affecting rigor mortis: (A) early onset and short duration (rapid ATP depletion) — high environmental temperature, exhaustion, fatigue, violent muscular activity or struggle before death, convulsions (strychnine poisoning, tetanus, epilepsy), electrocution, prolonged fever or septicaemia, chronic wasting diseases, emaciation, poor muscular development, infants and the elderly, death from burns (heat), cyanide poisoning; (B) delayed onset and longer duration — low environmental temperature (cold), healthy adults with good musculature, sudden death without preceding exhaustion (e.g., asphyxia, haemorrhage, head injury), pneumonia, CO poisoning; (C) rigor is absent in fetus < 7 months gestation (rare — weak muscle development). Conditions simulating rigor mortis: (1) heat stiffening — when a body is exposed to temperatures above 65 °C (burns, boiling water, fire), proteins coagulate and muscles contract and become rigid; flexor muscles being bulkier, the limbs flex producing the 'pugilistic attitude' (boxer's/fencing attitude); this rigidity persists till putrefaction; normal rigor does not develop; muscles are desiccated, coagulated and pink; (2) cold stiffening — when a body is exposed to freezing temperatures (below 0 °C to −5 °C), the body fluids and subcutaneous fat solidify, producing stiffness; joints when forcibly moved give a crackling sound due to breaking of ice in synovial fluid; on warming the body, the stiffness disappears and true rigor may then develop rapidly; (3) gas stiffening — during putrefaction, accumulation of gases in tissues produces a stiff, distended body (bloating) that may simulate rigor; associated with other signs of decomposition. Cadaveric spasm (instantaneous rigor, cataleptic rigidity): a rare condition in which a group of voluntary muscles (rarely the whole body) that were contracting during life at the time of death become stiff instantaneously at the moment of death, without passing through the stage of primary relaxation, and the stiffness passes imperceptibly into true rigor mortis and passes off with it; mechanism — not well understood; associated with sudden death in conditions of intense emotion, fear, excitement, exhaustion or violent muscular activity, nervous tension, injury to the brain or spinal cord (e.g., firearm wound of brain), and possibly local ATP depletion with neural impulses at the moment of death; examples — (a) suicide — the weapon (revolver, knife, razor) firmly grasped in the hand of the deceased (strong evidence of suicide, because the grip cannot be simulated after death — a weapon placed in the hand of a dead person by a murderer is held loosely); (b) drowning — grass, weeds, mud, sand or other objects firmly clutched in the hands indicating the person was alive on entering water (evidence of antemortem drowning); (c) homicide — hair, buttons, clothing pieces of the assailant clutched by the victim during the struggle (may help identify the assailant); (d) battlefield — soldiers found in the posture of firing/aiming; mountaineers in falls; persons in aircraft crashes holding seat arms; (e) the whole body may be involved (rare — e.g., soldiers shot in the head in battle). Medicolegal importance of cadaveric spasm: it records the last act of the person in life, indicating the manner of death (suicide, homicide, accident) and the circumstances, and it cannot be produced artificially after death. Medicolegal importance of rigor mortis: sign of death; helps estimate the time since death; indicates the position of the body at death — if the position of a rigid body does not conform to gravity (e.g., legs raised in air while lying flat), the body has been moved after rigor developed; may indicate the cause of death (e.g., strychnine — early, intense rigor; heat stiffening in burns).",
[["Rigor mortis vs cadaveric spasm",[["Feature","Rigor mortis","Cadaveric spasm"],["Onset","1–2 h after death (India)","Instantaneous, at moment of death"],["Primary relaxation","Precedes it","Absent"],["Muscles involved","All muscles (voluntary and involuntary)","Group of voluntary muscles (usually hand)"],["Mechanism","ATP depletion, actomyosin complex","Uncertain; nervous; intense emotion"],["Predisposing factors","None","Sudden violent death, fear, excitement, exhaustion"],["Stiffness","Moderate; overcome easily, doesn't return","Marked; great force needed"],["Artificial production","—","Cannot be produced after death"],["Medicolegal value","Time since death, position","Manner of death (suicide, drowning, struggle)"]]],
["Time course in India",[["Phase","Time"],["Primary relaxation","1–2 h"],["Onset","1–2 h (heart first; eyelids first visibly)"],["Fully developed","12 h"],["Persists","12 h"],["Passes off","24–36 h (summer), up to 48 h (winter)"]]],
["Factors affecting rigor",[["Early onset, short duration","Delayed onset, long duration"],["High temperature","Cold"],["Exhaustion, struggle, convulsions (strychnine, tetanus)","Healthy muscular adults"],["Electrocution, septicaemia","Sudden death (asphyxia, haemorrhage)"],["Emaciation, infants, elderly","—"]]],
["Conditions simulating rigor",[["Condition","Features"],["Heat stiffening","> 65 °C; protein coagulation; pugilistic attitude"],["Cold stiffening","Freezing of fluids/fat; crackling joints; disappears on warming"],["Gas stiffening","Putrefactive bloating"]]]],
{i:"Rigor mortis follows ATP depletion and proceeds in Nysten's order; cadaveric spasm freezes the last act of life.",x:[["Examples of cadaveric spasm",["Weapon firmly grasped — suicide","Weeds/grass clutched — antemortem drowning","Assailant's hair/clothing in victim's hand — homicide","Soldiers in firing posture"]]],f:"Death → ATP falls → actin–myosin locked → rigor (eyelids → face → neck → arms → trunk → legs) → autolysis breaks complex → secondary relaxation\n Violent death + intense emotion → instantaneous spasm (no primary relaxation) → merges with rigor",c:"Rigor mortis is generalised muscle stiffening from ATP depletion that in India begins at 1–2 hours, is complete by 12, lasts 12 and passes off by 24–36 hours in Nysten's order, aiding estimation of time since death; cadaveric spasm is instantaneous localised stiffening at death without primary relaxation that cannot be simulated and indicates the manner of death."});

Z("fmt4",4,"f",["Decomposition is the final post-mortem change in which body tissues break down by autolysis (self-digestion by cellular enzymes) and putrefaction (bacterial action, mainly Clostridium perfringens from the gut); in India it begins with greenish discolouration of the right iliac fossa at 12–18 h, followed by marbling, bloating and liquefaction; under special conditions it is modified into adipocere (saponification of fat in warm, moist conditions) or mummification (desiccation in hot, dry, airy conditions), both of which preserve features for identification.","Decomposition is the body being recycled — enzymes and gut bacteria digest it; moisture turns fat to soap (adipocere), dry heat turns it to leather (mummy).",["Autolysis: earliest — brain, pancreas, adrenal medulla, gastric mucosa (post-mortem softening), retina; aseptic chemical process; fetal maceration in utero.","Putrefaction sequence (India): green RIF (caecum close to skin; sulphmethaemoglobin) 12–18 h → spreads over abdomen 18–24 h → marbling (veins green-purple) 36–48 h → bloating, protrusion of eyes/tongue, purge fluid, skin blisters, slippage 24–48 h → maggots (24 h onward), loosening of hair/nails 48–72 h → colliquative stage (soft tissues liquefy) 5–10 days → skeletonisation weeks–months; Casper's dictum — air : water : soil = 1 : 2 : 8 (one week in air = 2 weeks in water = 8 weeks in soil).","Order of organ putrefaction: early — larynx/trachea, stomach, intestines, spleen, liver, brain; late — prostate (male), non-gravid uterus (female) — last; heart, kidneys, bladder intermediate; bones and teeth resist.","Adipocere (saponification; grave wax): hydrolysis and hydrogenation of body fat into fatty acids (oleic → palmitic, stearic, hydroxystearic) by lecithinase of C. perfringens in moist warm conditions; greyish-white, greasy, waxy, rancid smell, floats in water, burns with yellow flame; first in subcutaneous fat of cheeks, breasts, buttocks; begins ~3 weeks in India (3–12 weeks); preserves features and injuries.","Mummification: dehydration/desiccation of tissues in hot, dry, windy conditions (deserts, sealed rooms) with absent bacterial action; skin dry, leathery, brown-black, shrunken, odourless; complete in about 3 weeks–3 months in India; preserves identity, injuries (ligature marks)."],"Casper's dictum 1 : 2 : 8 (air : water : soil); green RIF first; prostate/uterus last."],
"Decomposition is the last of the post-mortem changes, by which the complex organic tissues of the body are broken down into simpler substances; it consists of two processes: (1) autolysis — self-digestion of tissues by their own intracellular enzymes (lysosomal hydrolases), occurring without bacterial involvement (aseptic); begins immediately after death; seen first and most marked in organs rich in enzymes or with high metabolic activity — pancreas (rapid autolysis), adrenal medulla, gastric and oesophageal mucosa (post-mortem softening — gastromalacia/oesophagomalacia — may mimic perforation), brain, retina (detached), liver; intrauterine death of the fetus results in maceration (aseptic autolysis — skin slippage, reddish-brown discolouration, soft, flattened skull, Spalding's sign) in 24–48 h; (2) putrefaction — bacterial decomposition of tissues, chiefly by anaerobic organisms from the intestine (Clostridium perfringens/welchii — most important, producing lecithinase; also coliforms, Streptococcus, Staphylococcus, Proteus, Bacillus) which invade tissues and blood vessels after death; bacteria produce enzymes that break down proteins, carbohydrates and fats; gases produced — hydrogen sulphide, methane, carbon dioxide, ammonia, hydrogen, phosphoretted hydrogen (inflammable), mercaptans; foul-smelling compounds — putrescine, cadaverine, indole, skatole; ptomaines. External signs of putrefaction (sequence in India; in temperate climates about twice as slow): (1) greenish discolouration of the skin of the right iliac fossa over the caecum (first external sign) — 12–18 hours in summer (24–48 h in winter); due to formation of sulphmethaemoglobin (H₂S combines with haemoglobin); the caecum is first because it is superficial and contains liquid contents full of bacteria; in drowning bodies, first appears on the face and neck; in newborns (sterile gut) — begins around nose/mouth/umbilicus; spreads over the whole abdomen, external genitalia, and chest within 24–36 h; (2) marbling — bacteria spread along superficial veins which become greenish-purple/black in an arborescent pattern (haemolysis and sulphmethaemoglobin) — seen on shoulders, upper chest, thighs, groins — 36–48 hours; (3) bloating (gaseous distension) — within 24–48 hours (summer) — gases accumulate in the intestines, tissues and cavities: face swollen and distorted (features unrecognisable), eyes protruding, lips swollen and everted, tongue protruding, abdomen distended, scrotum and penis swollen (may mimic erection), breasts swollen; froth and blood-stained fluid (purge fluid) escapes from mouth and nostrils (may simulate antemortem bleeding); faeces may be forced out, urine expelled; prolapse of rectum/uterus; expulsion of fetus from the uterus of a dead pregnant woman (post-mortem delivery, 'coffin birth'); (4) skin changes — epidermis loosens, forming blisters (bullae) containing reddish putrid fluid (2–3 days), skin slippage (peeling) on handling; degloving of hands and feet (skin of hands comes off as a glove — 3–5 days — can be used for fingerprints); hair and nails loosen (48–72 h onwards) and come off easily; (5) maggots — flies (blowflies — Calliphoridae; house flies) lay eggs in moist orifices (eyes, nostrils, mouth, ears, wounds) within minutes to hours after death in summer; eggs hatch into larvae (maggots) in 8–24 hours; larvae pass through three instar stages (~ 4–5 days), then pupate (4–5 days) and adult flies emerge (total egg–adult cycle ~ 10–14 days in summer) — basis of forensic entomology for estimating time since death; maggots can destroy soft tissues of an adult body in about a week; other fauna — beetles, ants, rodents, dogs, jackals, crabs, fish (in water); (6) colliquative putrefaction (liquefaction) — 5–10 days onwards — soft tissues become soft, pasty, and liquefy; abdomen bursts; cavities open; organs disintegrate; (7) skeletonisation — remains reduced to bones with ligaments and cartilage; in India, in the open, a body may become skeletonised in 2–4 weeks (faster with maggot and animal activity); in soil months to years. Internal changes: blood haemolyses and stains vessel walls and tissues (post-mortem staining of intima); organs soften and become discoloured; the order in which organs putrefy — early: larynx and trachea (lining), stomach, intestines, spleen (soft, diffluent), liver, brain (soft, pinkish-grey pasty mass), lungs, pregnant uterus; later: heart, kidneys, bladder, oesophagus, diaphragm; last: prostate (in males) and non-gravid uterus (in females) — most resistant soft tissues (useful for sexing); bones and teeth last indefinitely; foamy liver (honeycomb appearance due to gas — 'foamy organs'). Factors affecting putrefaction: (A) external — (1) temperature: optimum 21–38 °C; rapid in hot humid climate; retarded below 10 °C and arrested below 0 °C and above 48 °C; (2) moisture: essential for bacteria — rapid in moist air; (3) air: free access of air hastens (flies, oxygen); (4) medium — Casper's dictum: at the same temperature, a body decomposes in air twice as fast as in water and eight times as fast as in soil (1 week in air = 2 weeks in water = 8 weeks in earth); buried bodies — depth, type of soil (porous dry sandy soil delays; clay, moist soil intermediate), coffin; bodies in water — warmer stagnant/sewage water faster; fresh running cold water slower; bodies float up due to gas in 24–48 h (summer); (5) clothing — tight clothing retards initially; loose warm clothing hastens; (B) internal — (1) age: newborns (sterile) and stillborns putrefy slowly; children faster than elderly; (2) sex: no difference; (3) body build: fat bodies decompose faster (more fluid, heat retention) than lean; (4) cause of death: rapid in septicaemia, peritonitis, gangrene, infections, oedema, anasarca, asphyxia, deaths following prolonged illness, dropsy; delayed in deaths from haemorrhage, dehydration (cholera, diarrhoea), poisoning by arsenic, antimony, zinc chloride, strychnine, carbolic acid, cyanide (some), formalin, chronic alcoholism, heavy-metal poisoning (antiseptic effect), and embalmed bodies; (5) mutilation/injuries hasten (entry of bacteria). Modifications of decomposition: (I) adipocere (saponification; 'grave wax'; French adipo — fat, cire — wax): a modification in which the body fat is converted into a yellowish-white, greasy, waxy substance; mechanism — post-mortem hydrolysis of neutral fats (triglycerides) into free fatty acids (oleic acid, palmitic, stearic) by intrinsic lipases and bacterial lecithinase (Clostridium perfringens), followed by hydrogenation of unsaturated oleic acid into saturated hydroxystearic, palmitic and stearic acids; water from the tissues is needed initially, so body fluids or a moist environment are required; calcium and magnesium salts (soaps) form later in water with high mineral content; conditions favouring — warm, moist environment with absence of free air: bodies submerged in water (ponds, wells, sewers), buried in damp soil, sealed vaults/coffins, fat bodies, women and infants (more fat); appearance — greyish-white or yellowish, greasy, soft (later hard and brittle), waxy, translucent, with a sweetish rancid odour; floats in water, dissolves in alcohol and ether, melts on heating and burns with a yellow flame; sites — first in the subcutaneous fat of cheeks, breasts, buttocks, abdomen, limbs; later internal organs; entire body may be converted (rare); time — in India in summer, adipocere formation is first seen in about 3 days to 1 week in water (fastest reported), usually 2–3 weeks; complete conversion of a body takes months; it may persist for years or centuries; medicolegal importance — preserves the features of the face and body shape, permitting identification; preserves injuries (wounds, ligature marks, fractures); helps estimate time since death (weeks to months); indicates the conditions of disposal (water/damp burial); may preserve evidence of poisoning; (II) mummification: a modification in which the body dries up (desiccation/dehydration) and shrivels, with arrest of putrefaction; conditions favouring — dry, hot, warm air with free circulation (windy), absence of moisture: hot sandy soils and deserts (Rajasthan), sealed dry rooms or cupboards, hot climates in summer, burial in shallow dry graves with free air, bodies of newborns (sterile, small), emaciated bodies, chronic arsenic/antimony poisoning (retard putrefaction), dehydration before death; the body loses water rapidly by evaporation before bacteria can act; appearance — body is dried, shrunken, wrinkled, light in weight (loses ~ 60–70% weight), stiff, brittle; skin dry, leathery, hard, rusty brown to black, stretched tightly over bony prominences; internal organs shrunken into a brown-black mass; no offensive smell (odourless) in completed mummy; features (and injuries) are preserved; fungal growth may occur; time — partial mummification of fingers/toes in a few days; complete mummification of an adult body takes about 3 months (2–3 weeks in very hot dry conditions in India; faster in infants); a mummified body may persist for years; medicolegal importance — identification (features, tattoos, scars, fingerprints after rehydration), cause of death (injuries — fractures, ligature marks, stab wounds preserved), time since death; (III) other — maceration (aseptic autolysis of a dead fetus in utero — skin slippage in 12 h, reddish discolouration and bullae 24–48 h, Spalding's sign, mushy body after 1 week); freezing preserves the body. Medicolegal importance of decomposition: estimation of the time since death; identification becomes difficult (features distorted, skin colour changed, hair lost) — need for dental, DNA, fingerprints from degloved skin; artifacts must be distinguished from antemortem lesions — purge fluid from mouth (simulating bleeding/poisoning), bloating (simulating obesity), protrusion of tongue/eyes (simulating asphyxia), discolouration (simulating bruises), skin slippage (simulating burns), gas in tissues, splitting of skin (simulating wounds), postmortem fractures and separation of skull sutures (gas pressure — 'post-mortem extradural haemorrhage' heat effect), floating of bodies; poisons may be lost or produced (alcohol and cyanide produced in small amounts; CO, volatile poisons lost); exhumation decisions.",
[["Sequence of putrefaction (India, summer)",[["Change","Time"],["Green discolouration of right iliac fossa","12–18 h"],["Green over whole abdomen","18–24 h"],["Bloating, purge fluid, swollen face","24–48 h"],["Marbling","36–48 h"],["Blisters, skin slippage","48–72 h"],["Loosening of hair, nails; degloving","2–5 days"],["Colliquative liquefaction","5–10 days"],["Skeletonisation","2–4 weeks (open) to months"]]],
["Order of organ putrefaction",[["Early","Intermediate","Late"],["Larynx, trachea, stomach, intestines, spleen, liver, brain","Heart, lungs, kidneys, bladder","Prostate, non-gravid uterus; bones and teeth last"]]],
["Factors",[["Hasten","Retard"],["Heat (21–38 °C), moisture, air","Cold (< 10 °C), dryness"],["Fat body, infants (non-newborn)","Newborn, lean, elderly"],["Septicaemia, peritonitis, oedema","Haemorrhage, dehydration"],["Injuries","Arsenic, antimony, zinc chloride, strychnine, embalming"],["Air","Water (×2), soil (×8) — Casper's dictum"]]],
["Adipocere vs mummification",[["Feature","Adipocere","Mummification"],["Process","Hydrolysis + hydrogenation of fat (saponification)","Desiccation (dehydration)"],["Conditions","Warm, moist, no air (water, damp graves)","Hot, dry, airy (deserts, sealed dry rooms)"],["Appearance","Greyish-white, greasy, waxy, soft→brittle","Dry, leathery, brown-black, shrunken"],["Smell","Rancid, sweetish","Odourless"],["Sites first","Subcutaneous fat: cheeks, breasts, buttocks","Fingers, toes, face"],["Time (India)","~3 weeks (days to weeks)","~3 weeks–3 months"],["Value","Identity, injuries, PMI","Identity, injuries, PMI"]]]],
{i:"Decomposition is autolysis plus bacterial putrefaction; moisture leads to adipocere and dry heat to mummification.",x:[["Putrefactive artefacts",["Purge fluid simulating haemorrhage","Bloating simulating obesity","Discolouration simulating bruises","Skin slippage simulating burns","Protruding tongue/eyes simulating asphyxia","Splitting of skin simulating wounds"]]],f:"Death → autolysis (pancreas, adrenal, stomach) → gut bacteria (C. perfringens) invade → H₂S + Hb → sulphmethaemoglobin → green RIF → marbling → gas bloating → blisters → liquefaction → skeleton\n Moist warm, no air → adipocere; Hot dry airy → mummification",c:"Decomposition by autolysis and putrefaction begins with green discolouration of the right iliac fossa at 12–18 hours in India and progresses through marbling, bloating and liquefaction to skeletonisation, governed by temperature, moisture and Casper's dictum; adipocere (saponification in moist conditions) and mummification (desiccation in dry heat) arrest decay and preserve identity and injuries."});

Z("fmt4",5,"f",["Time since death (post-mortem interval, PMI) is estimated by combining several methods because no single method is precise: in the early period (first 24–36 h) — body cooling (algor mortis), lividity, rigor mortis, eye changes, vitreous potassium and stomach contents; in the late period — stage of decomposition, adipocere, mummification and forensic entomology (maggot development); the answer is always given as a range.","Each post-mortem change ticks at its own speed — reading several clocks together gives a reliable window, never an exact minute.",["Algor mortis: body cools ~0.75–1 °F/h (≈ 0.4–0.5 °C/h) in temperate climates after an initial plateau (0.5–1 h); rectal temperature; formula — PMI (h) = (98.4 °F − rectal temp °F) / 1.5 (Indian: /1 to /1.5); Henssge nomogram (body weight, ambient temperature, clothing); in India cooling slow in summer.","Lividity (appears 1–2 h, fixed 6–8 h), rigor (onset 1–2 h, full 12 h, gone 24–36 h), eye changes (corneal clouding 2 h, tache noire 3–6 h), stomach contents (light meal empties 2–3 h, heavy 4–6 h), bladder, beard growth (0.4 mm/day — unreliable).","Chemical: vitreous potassium rises linearly (Sturner: PMI h = 7.14 × K⁺ mEq/L − 39.1); CSF/blood changes (lactic acid, NPN, hypoxanthine); supravital reactions — electrical excitability of muscles (~6 h), pupillary response to drugs (~4 h), idiomuscular contraction.","Late: putrefaction stages (green RIF 12–18 h, bloating 24–48 h, marbling 36–48 h, maggots, liquefaction), adipocere (weeks), mummification (weeks–months), skeletonisation; entomology (eggs 8–24 h hatch, larvae 3 instars ~4–5 days, pupae, adult 10–14 days); bones — soft tissue tags, smell, marrow, UV fluorescence, nitrogen content, radiocarbon (> 50 yr); plant roots, circumstantial evidence (newspapers, watch, food)."],"Rough rule (India): warm + flaccid → < 3 h; warm + stiff → 3–8 h; cold + stiff → 8–36 h; cold + flaccid + green → > 36 h."],
"Estimation of time since death (post-mortem interval — PMI) is one of the most important and difficult questions in forensic practice; it helps to (a) verify or refute alibis and statements of suspects and witnesses, (b) narrow the period during which the crime occurred, (c) identify the deceased (matching with missing persons), (d) settle civil matters — inheritance (who died first — survivorship), insurance, pension; accuracy decreases as the interval increases. Methods (combined): (A) Early post-mortem period (up to 24–72 h): (1) body cooling — algor mortis: after death heat production ceases and the body cools by conduction, convection, radiation and evaporation towards the ambient temperature; the fall is sigmoid — initial temperature plateau (0.5–1 h up to 3–5 h) due to continuing metabolism and the time needed to establish a temperature gradient, then a fairly linear fall, then a slower terminal phase; measured by rectal temperature (chemical thermometer 25 cm long inserted 8–10 cm, or thermocouple probe), or by temperature of liver (subhepatic), brain (nasal), ear canal; skin cooling is unreliable; rate of cooling in temperate climates — about 1 °F (0.55 °C) per hour (0.75–1.0 °F/h) — in India, standard approximations: rate ~ 1 °F/h in winter and less in summer (body may not cool at all if ambient temperature ≥ body temperature); formula — PMI (hours) = (normal body temperature 98.4 °F − rectal temperature °F) / rate of fall per hour (1.5 °F used in many Indian texts); Henssge's nomogram (1988) — the most accurate standardised method, using rectal temperature, ambient temperature and body weight with corrective factors for clothing, covering, wind, water; gives a 95% confidence interval (± 2.8 h); factors affecting cooling — ambient temperature (most important), body build (fat insulates; children, thin bodies cool faster), clothing and coverings, posture (extended cools faster than curled), air movement, humidity, immersion in water (cools faster, especially running water), the initial body temperature at death (raised in fever, heat stroke, pontine haemorrhage, strangulation, septicaemia, tetanus, strychnine and some poisons, exercise; lowered in hypothermia, massive haemorrhage, shock, congestive heart failure, alcohol); post-mortem caloricity — rise of temperature after death in the first 2 hours in cholera, septicaemia, tetanus, strychnine poisoning, heat stroke, pontine haemorrhage (glycogenolysis, bacterial activity, convulsions); (2) post-mortem lividity — appears 30 min–2 h, well developed 6 h, fixed 6–8 h (blanchable before, shifting before fixation); (3) rigor mortis — begins 1–2 h, fully developed 12 h, persists 12 h, passes off in 12 h (24–36 h total in India; slower in winter); (4) changes in the eye — loss of corneal reflex (immediate), segmentation of retinal vessels (minutes), fall of IOP (2 h), corneal haziness (within 2 h, opaque by 6–12 h with lids closed), tache noire (3–6 h), progressive fundoscopic changes (pallor of disc, fragmentation of vessels); (5) chemical changes — vitreous humour potassium: after death potassium diffuses from retinal and choroidal cells into the vitreous and its concentration rises linearly with time for up to ~ 100 hours; formula (Sturner, 1963): PMI (h) = 7.14 × K⁺ (mEq/L) − 39.1; Madea's formula: PMI (h) = 5.26 × K⁺ − 30.9; accuracy ± 10–20 h; vitreous is relatively protected from contamination and putrefaction; also hypoxanthine; CSF — lactic acid, non-protein nitrogen, amino acids, potassium rise; blood — changes unreliable; synovial fluid potassium; (6) supravital reactions (molecular life) — mechanical excitability of skeletal muscles (idiomuscular contraction — a tap with a reflex hammer produces a localised swelling/bulge — up to 1.5–2.5 h; Zsako's phenomenon), electrical excitability of facial muscles (eyelids, mouth — up to ~ 6–8 h), pharmacological reactivity of iris (pupil dilates with atropine/tropicamide or constricts with pilocarpine injected subconjunctivally — up to ~ 4–5 h (14–46 h in some studies)), sweat gland response to pilocarpine; (7) stomach contents — the stage of digestion of the last meal (if the time of the last meal is known): light meal leaves the stomach in about 2–3 hours, a moderate meal 3–4 h, heavy meal 4–6 h; if the stomach contains undigested food — death within about 2 h of the meal; partly digested — 2–4 h; empty stomach — at least 4–6 h after the meal (but highly variable — emotional stress, illness, head injury, drugs, alcohol and the nature of the food affect gastric emptying; unreliable alone); food in small intestine; (8) urinary bladder — full bladder suggests death in the early hours of the morning (night sleep) — weak evidence; (9) growth of hair — beard grows about 0.4–0.5 mm per day; apparent post-mortem growth is due to skin retraction from drying (not true growth); (10) cellular changes — microscopic, enzymatic changes, flow cytometry of DNA degradation, RNA degradation (research). (B) Late post-mortem period: (1) putrefaction — green discolouration of right iliac fossa (12–18 h in summer), green over abdomen and chest (18–24 h), bloating and purge fluid (24–48 h), marbling (36–48 h), blisters and skin slippage (48–72 h), loosening of hair and nails, degloving (3–5 days), liquefaction (5–10 days), skeletonisation (weeks–months); timing depends heavily on temperature, humidity, medium (Casper's dictum 1 : 2 : 8 — air : water : soil); in water — bodies surface after 1–3 days in summer; washerwoman's hands (wrinkling of palms and soles — 3–4 h in water; skin peeling 1–2 days), floating; (2) adipocere — first noticed after ~ 3 days to 3 weeks in favourable conditions in India; widespread in weeks–months; (3) mummification — partial in weeks; complete in about 3 months (faster in deserts and infants); (4) forensic entomology — study of insects (mainly blowflies — Calliphora, Lucilia, Chrysomya; house flies; flesh flies — Sarcophaga; beetles in later stages) colonising the body in a predictable succession; flies are attracted within minutes and lay eggs in natural orifices and wounds (not at night usually); eggs hatch into first-instar larvae in 8–24 h; larvae moult to second instar (~ 1–2 days) and third instar (~ 3–4 days), then migrate away and pupate (5–7 days), and adult flies emerge (~ 10–14 days, depending on temperature — accumulated degree days/hours); the minimum PMI is estimated from the age of the oldest larvae/pupae (length, instar, crop) in relation to temperature records; insect succession (sequence of species — blowflies, then flesh flies, beetles, mites) helps in longer intervals (weeks–months); entomotoxicology — drugs/poisons detected in maggots; also indicates whether body has been moved (species not native to area), wounds (unusual egg sites), antemortem myiasis (neglect); (5) skeletal remains — estimation becomes very inexact: fresh bones with soft tissue, ligaments, periosteum, smell, greasy marrow and fat — less than a year (in India within months); dry, light, brittle bones without odour, cartilage or marrow — several years; weathering (cracking, flaking, bleaching by sun), root marks and plant growth (age of roots growing through bones/clothes), deposition of minerals, animal gnawing; laboratory tests — fluorescence under ultraviolet light (fresh bone fluoresces silvery-blue across the cut surface; fluorescence diminishes from outside inwards and disappears after ~ 100–150 years), nitrogen content (falls with time; > 4% suggests < 350 years), amino acid content (proline, hydroxyproline), benzidine/luminol reaction for blood (positive up to ~ 100 years), indophenol test, radiocarbon dating (¹⁴C — atomic bomb peak (1955–63) helps date bones of last 60 years), strontium-90, lead-210/polonium-210 ratio; (6) clothing, jewellery and objects — decay of fabrics (natural fibres decay, synthetics persist), rusting of metals, dates on newspapers, letters, receipts, food containers; mail accumulation, uncollected milk/newspapers, last phone call/message, electronic data (mobile phone records, CCTV, social media activity, smart watches); stopped watches (unreliable); state of the scene (lights on, meals). General rough rule for Indian conditions (approximate): body warm and flaccid — less than 3 hours; body warm and stiff — 3–8 hours; body cold and stiff — 8–36 hours; body cold and flaccid with greenish discolouration — more than 36 hours (Indian texts give 12–24–36 h variants). Opinion should be stated as a range ('about 12–18 hours before the post-mortem examination') considering all findings, the environmental conditions, and the inherent biological variation, and the time of the post-mortem examination should be noted.",
[["Early methods",[["Method","Principle / time"],["Algor mortis","~1 °F/h after plateau; PMI = (98.4 − rectal °F)/1.5; Henssge nomogram"],["Lividity","Appears 30 min–2 h; fixed 6–8 h"],["Rigor mortis","Onset 1–2 h; full 12 h; lasts 12 h; gone 24–36 h"],["Eye changes","Cattle-trucking (minutes), corneal clouding (2 h), tache noire (3–6 h)"],["Vitreous potassium","Linear rise; PMI = 7.14 × K⁺ − 39.1 (Sturner)"],["Supravital reactions","Muscle excitability (up to 6–8 h), pupil drug response (~4–5 h)"],["Stomach contents","Light meal 2–3 h; heavy 4–6 h to empty"]]],
["Late methods",[["Method","Time"],["Green right iliac fossa","12–18 h"],["Bloating, marbling","24–48 h"],["Blisters, slippage, maggots","2–3 days"],["Liquefaction","5–10 days"],["Adipocere","Weeks"],["Mummification","Weeks–3 months"],["Skeletonisation","Weeks–months"],["Entomology","Eggs hatch 8–24 h; adult fly 10–14 days"],["Bones","UV fluorescence, nitrogen, ¹⁴C dating"]]],
["Rough rule (India)",[["Findings","PMI"],["Warm, flaccid","< 3 h"],["Warm, stiff","3–8 h"],["Cold, stiff","8–36 h"],["Cold, flaccid, green RIF","> 36 h"]]],
["Factors causing error",["Ambient temperature, clothing, body build","Fever, hypothermia at death","Post-mortem caloricity (cholera, tetanus, strychnine, heat stroke, septicaemia)","Immersion in water","Variable gastric emptying"]]],
{i:"PMI is estimated by integrating multiple post-mortem changes and environmental factors and expressed as a range.",x:[["Post-mortem caloricity",["Temperature rises after death for ~2 h","Cholera, septicaemia, tetanus, strychnine, heat stroke, pontine haemorrhage"]],["Forensic entomology",["Blowflies lay eggs in orifices/wounds","Larval instars, pupae, adult — temperature dependent","Minimum PMI; movement of body; entomotoxicology"]]],f:"0–24 h: temperature + lividity + rigor + eyes + vitreous K⁺ + stomach\n 24–72 h: rigor resolving + putrefaction stages + maggots\n Weeks: adipocere / mummification / entomology succession\n Months–years: skeletal condition, UV fluorescence, ¹⁴C, circumstantial evidence",c:"Time since death is estimated as a range by combining early changes — cooling, lividity, rigor, eye changes, vitreous potassium, supravital reactions and gastric contents — with late changes of decomposition, adipocere, mummification, entomology and skeletal features, always accounting for environmental and individual factors."});

Z("fmt4",6,"f",["A medicolegal post-mortem examination (autopsy) is a scientific examination of a dead body ordered by police or magistrate (S. 194/196 BNSS) to establish identity, cause of death, manner of death and time since death and to collect evidence; it is conducted by a registered medical officer in daylight (night PMs now permitted with videography and adequate lighting except in homicide, suicide, rape, decomposed and foul-play cases), with inquest papers, external and internal examination of all three cavities, preservation of viscera, and a written report; exhumation is the lawful digging out of a buried body on a magistrate's order for examination.","The autopsy lets the dead 'speak' — through a systematic head-to-toe examination the doctor answers who, why, how and when.",["Types: clinical (pathological — with relatives' consent, to study disease) vs medicolegal (forensic — on police/magistrate requisition, no consent needed); psychological autopsy; virtual autopsy (virtopsy — CT/MRI).","Rules: written requisition + inquest report; identification by police/relatives; conduct as soon as possible; daylight (MoHFW 2021 allows night PM with video recording, except homicide, suicide, rape, decomposed, suspected foul play unless law and order situation); body identified by police; no unauthorised persons; record all findings; photograph injuries; complete examination of three cavities even if cause apparent; preserve viscera when poisoning suspected; give opinion; hand over body; PM report copies.","Procedure: external examination (clothes, identity marks, PM changes, injuries, orifices), internal — incisions (I-shaped — chin to pubis; Y-shaped; modified Y; T-shaped; head — intermastoid coronal incision), removal of organs (Virchow — organ by organ; Rokitansky — in situ; Ghon — en bloc in systems; Letulle — en masse), examine each organ, histopathology, microbiology, toxicology samples.","Objectives: identity, cause of death, manner (natural, accidental, suicidal, homicidal), time since death, live birth/viability in newborns, whether injuries AM or PM, weapon, collection of trace evidence, retain evidence; negative autopsy (~5% — no cause found: functional deaths — vagal inhibition, epilepsy, arrhythmia, electrocution without marks, some poisons, hypoglycaemia, anaphylaxis)."],"Medicolegal autopsy: no consent needed, on requisition; examine all 3 cavities."],
"Autopsy (Greek — 'autos' self, 'opsis' view — 'to see for oneself'; also necropsy, post-mortem examination) is a detailed examination of a dead body, external and internal, to determine the cause of death and other facts. Types: (1) clinical/pathological/academic autopsy — performed in hospitals by pathologists with written consent of the next of kin, to find out the cause of death, extent of disease, effects of treatment, for medical education, audit and research; (2) medicolegal (forensic) autopsy — performed on the written requisition of the police (S. 194 BNSS — police inquest) or magistrate (S. 196 BNSS — magistrate inquest) in cases of sudden, suspicious, unnatural or violent deaths and medicolegal cases; consent of relatives is not required; (3) psychological autopsy — reconstruction of the psychological state of the deceased before death (suicide/equivocal deaths) by interviews with relatives and review of records; (4) virtual autopsy (virtopsy) — non-invasive post-mortem imaging by multi-slice CT, MRI, 3D surface scanning, angiography — useful where religious objections exist, for documenting fractures, gas embolism, gunshot trajectories (MoHFW/AIIMS virtual autopsy facility since 2020); (5) minimally invasive autopsy (needle biopsies + imaging); (6) second autopsy (repeat autopsy) on court order when the first is disputed; (7) exhumation autopsy. Objectives of a medicolegal autopsy: (a) to establish the identity of the deceased (when unknown); (b) to determine the cause of death (the disease or injury responsible); (c) to determine the manner (mode) of death — natural, accidental, suicidal or homicidal (or undetermined); (d) to estimate the time since death; (e) in newborns — whether born alive, viability, age, cause of death (infanticide); (f) to determine whether injuries are antemortem or postmortem, their age, the type of weapon used, and the number of assailants; (g) to collect, preserve and hand over evidence — viscera, blood, foreign bodies (bullets), trace evidence (hair, fibres, semen), clothing, for laboratory analyses; (h) to document findings in a report and give expert opinion in court; (i) to detect public health threats (infectious diseases, epidemics, occupational hazards, product defects). Rules (guidelines) for conducting a medicolegal autopsy: (1) performed only on receiving a written requisition from the police officer/magistrate along with the inquest report (panchnama), brief history, and relevant documents (hospital records, MLC papers); (2) performed by a registered medical practitioner authorised by the government (preferably a forensic medicine specialist; complicated cases — custodial deaths, dowry deaths, police firing — by a board of doctors and video-graphed as per NHRC guidelines); (3) the body should be identified by the police constable who accompanied it and by relatives (identification marks noted); photographs taken; in unknown bodies — fingerprints, photographs, DNA samples, dental chart; (4) should be performed as early as possible after receiving the body (to avoid post-mortem changes that destroy evidence) and without unnecessary delay; (5) timing — traditionally only in daylight because colour changes (bruises, jaundice, lividity) are better appreciated in natural light; MoHFW (Office Memorandum, 15 November 2021) permits post-mortem examination after sunset in hospitals with adequate infrastructure (lighting, video recording) for all cases except those of homicide, suicide, rape, decomposed bodies and suspicious foul play, unless there is a law-and-order situation; video recording is mandatory for night PMs; (6) no unauthorised person should be present; relatives may be allowed in certain cases (as per NHRC — custodial deaths, may be represented); the police officer may be present; medical students for teaching with consent of the authority; (7) the autopsy must be complete — all three cavities (cranial, thoracic, abdominal) must be opened and examined even if the cause of death appears obvious from external examination (to exclude other causes, contributory factors and to be able to answer questions in court); spinal cord, neck structures (layer-by-layer dissection in asphyxial deaths), limbs (for deep bruises) when indicated; (8) findings recorded at the time (notes, diagrams, photographs) — negative findings also recorded; (9) in suspected poisoning, appropriate viscera and samples preserved and sent to the chemical examiner (FSL); in other cases, samples for histopathology, microbiology, DNA, diatoms; (10) after the autopsy the body is properly reconstructed (stitched, cleaned) and handed over to the police for handing over to relatives with dignity; (11) the post-mortem report is prepared promptly (preferably immediately), in prescribed form with clear description and opinion; copies to police; the doctor should not give information to the press; (12) chain of custody maintained for all samples and articles; (13) hazards — universal precautions for infection (HIV, HBV, HCV, TB, prions — high-risk autopsies), formalin exposure. Procedure: (A) preliminaries — check papers, identification, history, scene photographs; (B) external examination — clothing (description, tears, stains, cuts correlating with injuries — removed carefully, dried and preserved), general description — sex, age, height, weight, build, nutrition, complexion, identification marks (moles, scars, tattoos), deformities; post-mortem changes (temperature, rigor, lividity — distribution and colour, decomposition); eyes (conjunctival petechiae, cornea), face, natural orifices (mouth, nose, ears, anus, vagina — discharge, foreign bodies, injuries), neck (ligature marks), nails (cyanosis, scrapings), hands (cadaveric spasm, defence wounds), external injuries — each described, numbered, measured and located (type, size, shape, direction, age, ante-/post-mortem), X-rays where needed (bullets, fractures, infants); (C) internal examination — incisions: (i) 'I-shaped' incision — straight midline from chin to symphysis pubis avoiding umbilicus — most common; (ii) 'Y-shaped' incision — from each acromion/behind the ear along clavicle to the sternal notch, then midline to pubis — better cosmetic result, good for neck dissection; (iii) modified Y; (iv) 'T-shaped' incision — transverse across clavicles with midline; (v) for the head — coronal (intermastoid) incision over the vertex from one mastoid process to the other, reflecting the scalp forwards and backwards; skull cap removed by sawing circumferentially; spinal cord via posterior approach; methods of organ removal: Virchow's technique — organs removed one by one and examined (common in India); Rokitansky's technique — in situ dissection with partial removal (in infectious bodies); Ghon's technique — removal of organs in organ blocks/systems (thoracic block, coeliac block, urogenital block); Letulle's technique — en masse removal of thoracic, cervical, abdominal and pelvic organs as one block, then dissected (best for preserving relationships; done by forensic pathologists); each organ examined (weighed, sectioned), cavities inspected for fluid, blood (measured), adhesions; special techniques — air embolism (heart opened under water), pneumothorax (water test), layer-wise neck dissection after removal of brain and thoracic organs (bloodless field), stomach contents (smell, nature); (D) collection of samples — viscera for chemical analysis (stomach and contents, small intestine with contents, liver, kidney, blood, urine), histopathology (10% formalin), microbiology, vitreous, bile, hair/nails (chronic poisoning), diatoms (drowning — bone marrow), DNA (blood on FTA card, muscle, tooth, bone); (E) reconstruction of body; (F) report and opinion — cause of death in clear medical terms, pending reports (chemical analysis, histopathology) — opinion reserved and given after receipt. Negative (obscure) autopsy: about 2–10% of autopsies where gross and microscopic examination and toxicology fail to reveal a cause of death; reasons — inadequate history, improper/incomplete examination, lack of laboratory analysis, decomposition, and functional causes of death leaving no structural findings: vagal inhibition (reflex cardiac arrest), cardiac arrhythmias (long QT, Brugada — molecular autopsy/genetic testing), epilepsy (SUDEP), anaphylaxis, hypoglycaemia (insulin), diabetic coma, electrocution without marks, drowning in some cases (dry drowning), hypothermia, laryngeal spasm, status thymicolymphaticus (obsolete), poisoning with substances not detected (anaesthetics, insulin, potassium, succinylcholine, some plant poisons), concussion, SIDS in infants; 'obscure autopsy' — findings minimal or non-specific requiring further investigation. Exhumation: the lawful digging out of an already buried body from the grave for medicolegal examination; ordered only by a magistrate (District Magistrate/SDM/Executive Magistrate — S. 196 BNSS / 176(3) CrPC); there is no time limit in India (in some countries there is — e.g., France 10 years); indications — (a) criminal cases: when foul play is suspected after burial — homicide, poisoning, criminal abortion, death due to negligence, to identify a body, to re-examine in disputed first autopsy; (b) civil cases: accidental death claims, insurance, workmen's compensation, survivorship, disputed identity, inheritance, professional negligence; procedure — the magistrate issues order to the medical officer; exhumation is done in the presence of the magistrate, medical officer, police and identifiers (relatives) preferably in daylight (early morning); the grave is identified by relatives, cemetery keeper/register; earth is removed carefully; soil samples (about 500 g) from above, below and both sides of the body and a control sample from a distance (to rule out contamination in poisoning, especially arsenic and heavy metals), the coffin, shroud, and any fluids are collected; the body is identified; examination may be done at the site or the body transported to the mortuary; disinfectants should not be sprayed on the body; workers wear protective gear; findings recorded; samples for chemical analysis — viscera, hair, nails, bones (arsenic, antimony, lead, mercury, thallium persist for years; organic poisons less useful); medicolegal value — fractures and injuries to bones, metallic poisons, bullets, identity (teeth, bones), age, sex may still be determined even after years; adipocere/mummification may preserve soft tissue findings.",
[["Clinical vs medicolegal autopsy",[["Feature","Clinical","Medicolegal"],["Purpose","Disease, education, research","Cause, manner, time of death; identity; evidence"],["Ordered by","Physician with relatives' consent","Police (S. 194 BNSS) / magistrate (S. 196)"],["Consent","Required","Not required"],["Performed by","Pathologist","Authorised RMP / forensic expert"],["Extent","As consented","Complete — all three cavities"]]],
["Rules of medicolegal autopsy",["Written requisition + inquest report","Identification by police/relatives; photographs","Early; daylight (night PM permitted with video, except homicide, suicide, rape, decomposed, foul play)","No unauthorised persons","Complete examination of all three cavities","Record findings; photographs; X-rays","Preserve viscera/samples; chain of custody","Reconstruct body; hand over to police","Prompt report; opinion reserved pending reports"]],
["Techniques",[["Incision/method","Description"],["I-shaped","Chin to pubis (common)"],["Y-shaped","Shoulders to sternum, then midline (neck dissection)"],["Intermastoid coronal","Scalp for skull opening"],["Virchow","Organs removed one by one"],["Rokitansky","In situ dissection"],["Ghon","En bloc (organ systems)"],["Letulle","En masse (all organs together)"]]],
["Negative autopsy causes",["Vagal inhibition, arrhythmias (channelopathies)","Epilepsy (SUDEP), anaphylaxis","Hypoglycaemia, insulin, potassium, succinylcholine","Electrocution without marks, dry drowning, hypothermia","Inadequate history/examination/tests"]],
["Exhumation",["Order: magistrate only (S. 196 BNSS); no time limit in India","Presence: magistrate, doctor, police, relatives","Soil samples above, below, sides + control (metallic poisons)","Identify grave, coffin, body","Value: metallic poisons, fractures, bullets, identity"]]],
{c:"A medicolegal autopsy, performed on police or magistrate requisition without need for consent, systematically examines the body externally and all three cavities internally to determine identity, cause, manner and time of death and to preserve evidence, following rules on requisition, identification, completeness, timing and reporting; exhumation of a buried body is permissible only on a magistrate's order."});

Z("fmt4",7,"f",["Sudden natural death is an unexpected death from natural causes occurring within 24 hours (often within 1 hour — sudden cardiac death) of the onset of symptoms in an apparently healthy person; most are due to cardiovascular disease (coronary artery disease ~ 50–60%), then respiratory, CNS and other causes; custodial death is any death of a person in the custody of police or judicial authorities, which requires mandatory magistrate's (judicial) inquest, NHRC intimation within 24 hours and a video-recorded autopsy by a board of doctors.","Sudden natural death is the heart (usually) giving way without warning; custodial death is any death under the State's control — the law demands extra scrutiny because the victim cannot speak for himself.",["Causes of sudden natural death: CVS (most common) — coronary atherosclerosis/MI, arrhythmias, hypertensive heart disease, cardiomyopathies (HCM in young athletes), myocarditis, aortic stenosis, ruptured aortic aneurysm/dissection, pulmonary embolism, channelopathies (long QT, Brugada); respiratory — massive haemoptysis, asthma, pneumonia, laryngeal oedema; CNS — intracranial haemorrhage (SAH from berry aneurysm, hypertensive ICH), epilepsy (SUDEP), meningitis; GIT — haematemesis, perforated ulcer, acute pancreatitis; GUT — ruptured ectopic pregnancy, eclampsia; others — anaphylaxis, diabetic ketoacidosis, adrenal haemorrhage (Waterhouse–Friderichsen).","Medicolegal importance: suspected foul play or poisoning → autopsy; differentiation from violent death; insurance, workmen's compensation; death following trivial injury/emotional stress; deaths during sexual intercourse, in custody; negative autopsy.","Custodial death: in police lock-up, during interrogation, in prison/jail, judicial custody, juvenile homes, psychiatric institutions; causes — torture (injuries, kidney failure from rhabdomyolysis), suicide (hanging), natural disease, neglect of medical care, firearm (encounter); 'positional asphyxia', restraint deaths.","Procedure: magistrate (judicial) inquiry — S. 196(2) BNSS (176(1A) CrPC); NHRC informed within 24 h; autopsy by board of ≥ 2 doctors, video-filmed, NHRC model autopsy form; reports sent to NHRC; torture signs (falanga — soles beating, telefono, electric marks, ruler/rollers, suspension); Supreme Court D.K. Basu guidelines (1997) on arrest — medical examination every 48 h, inspection memo; BNSS S. 53 — examination of arrested person."],"Sudden death: CVS > respiratory > CNS. Custodial death: judicial inquiry + NHRC within 24 h + videographed board autopsy."],
"Sudden natural death: WHO defines sudden death as death occurring within 24 hours of the onset of symptoms; in forensic practice, a sudden death is one that is unexpected, occurs rapidly (often instantaneous or within minutes to hours) in an apparently healthy individual or in one with a known disease not expected to cause death so quickly; sudden cardiac death — natural death from cardiac causes within 1 hour of onset of symptoms; such deaths are medicolegally important because they are unexpected and may raise suspicion of foul play (poisoning, violence), especially when they occur in suspicious circumstances (in custody, at work, during sport, during sexual activity, after a quarrel or minor injury, in hotel rooms, in young persons); they constitute a substantial proportion of medicolegal autopsies. Causes of sudden natural death (in order of frequency): (A) cardiovascular system (most common — about 45–60%): (1) coronary artery disease — coronary atherosclerosis (most common single cause; > 75% narrowing of one or more major coronary arteries; left anterior descending — 'widow-maker'), acute coronary thrombosis, myocardial infarction (death often from ventricular fibrillation before infarction is visible — early MI shows no gross change; histology/TTC staining), rupture of infarct (haemopericardium, cardiac tamponade 3–7 days post-MI), papillary muscle rupture; coronary artery anomalies, coronary artery spasm (cocaine), dissection; (2) hypertensive heart disease with left ventricular hypertrophy (arrhythmias); (3) cardiomyopathies — hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (most common cause of sudden death in young athletes; asymmetric septal hypertrophy, myofibre disarray), dilated cardiomyopathy, arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (fibrofatty replacement), restrictive; (4) myocarditis (viral — young adults), rheumatic heart disease (mitral stenosis), aortic valve stenosis (calcific — exertional death), mitral valve prolapse, infective endocarditis; (5) aortic aneurysm rupture (atherosclerotic abdominal aneurysm, syphilitic thoracic aneurysm) and aortic dissection (Marfan syndrome, hypertension) — haemopericardium/haemothorax; (6) pulmonary thromboembolism (deep vein thrombosis after surgery, immobilisation, pregnancy, injury of legs — medicolegal sequelae of trauma); (7) congenital heart disease; (8) conduction system disorders and channelopathies (long QT syndrome, Brugada syndrome, CPVT, WPW) — 'sudden arrhythmic death syndrome' with structurally normal heart — molecular autopsy (genetic testing) recommended; commotio cordis; (9) cardiac tumours (myxoma). (B) respiratory system (~ 15–20%): massive haemoptysis (pulmonary tuberculosis, bronchiectasis, carcinoma), acute severe asthma (status asthmaticus), lobar pneumonia and bronchopneumonia (especially in alcoholics, elderly), acute epiglottitis and laryngeal oedema, diphtheria, spontaneous pneumothorax (tension), pulmonary oedema, aspiration of vomit (in intoxication), obstruction by tumours. (C) central nervous system (~ 10–18%): intracranial haemorrhage — spontaneous subarachnoid haemorrhage from rupture of a berry (saccular) aneurysm at the circle of Willis (young adults; may be precipitated by exertion, emotion, alcohol, minor trauma — medicolegal problem of 'trauma vs natural'), hypertensive intracerebral haemorrhage (basal ganglia, pons, cerebellum), haemorrhage into tumours, arteriovenous malformations; cerebral thrombosis/embolism (less frequently sudden); epilepsy — sudden unexpected death in epilepsy (SUDEP) — may leave no findings (tongue bite marks), status epilepticus; meningitis (meningococcal — with Waterhouse–Friderichsen adrenal haemorrhage), encephalitis, brain abscess; colloid cyst of third ventricle (acute hydrocephalus); brain tumours. (D) gastrointestinal system (~ 6–8%): massive haematemesis from peptic ulcer or oesophageal varices (cirrhosis), perforated peptic ulcer with peritonitis, acute haemorrhagic pancreatitis (alcoholics — fat necrosis; may simulate poisoning), intestinal obstruction (volvulus, strangulated hernia, intussusception), mesenteric thrombosis, ruptured liver/spleen (malaria, leukaemia — spontaneous or trivial trauma), acute fatty liver. (E) genitourinary system (~ 2–5%): ruptured ectopic pregnancy (tubal — most common cause of sudden death in young women of reproductive age with haemoperitoneum), eclampsia, amniotic fluid embolism, postpartum haemorrhage, uterine rupture, septic abortion; chronic renal failure (uraemia), Wilms tumour haemorrhage, cystic disease. (F) miscellaneous: anaphylaxis (drugs — penicillin, NSAIDs, contrast; insect stings — laryngeal oedema), diabetes mellitus (ketoacidosis, hypoglycaemia), Addison's disease, adrenal haemorrhage (Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome), thyrotoxic crisis, sickle cell crisis, haemophilia, leukaemia, heat stroke (environmental), status thymicolymphaticus (outdated concept), sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS — infants 1 month–1 year, during sleep, no cause found after complete investigation), alcoholism (fatty liver, cardiomyopathy, ketoacidosis), drug abuse. Medicolegal importance: (1) exclusion of foul play — poisoning (e.g., organophosphates, aluminium phosphide, cyanide, arsenic), smothering, strangulation; requires complete autopsy with toxicology and histopathology; (2) distinguishing whether death was caused/accelerated by injury, emotional stress or physical exertion (e.g., a quarrel or minor assault precipitating death from coronary disease or rupture of a berry aneurysm or a diseased spleen — the assailant's liability: 'eggshell skull rule' — take the victim as you find him; courts consider whether the injury was sufficient to cause death in the ordinary course of nature and the knowledge of the accused); (3) insurance claims (accident vs disease), workmen's compensation (death 'arising out of and in the course of employment'), compensation in sports; (4) deaths in custody, during medical/surgical procedures, during sexual intercourse (coital death — usually cardiac or intracranial haemorrhage), in hotels, in public places; (5) unattended deaths — certification; (6) public health — detection of infectious diseases, genetic disorders (screen relatives — channelopathies, HCM); (7) negative autopsy (functional causes). Custodial death: death of a person while in the custody of the police (police lock-up, during arrest, transport, interrogation, encounter) or judicial custody (prison/jail, under-trials, convicts), or in other state custody (juvenile homes, protective homes, beggar homes, mental hospitals, immigration detention); in India, NHRC reports many custodial deaths annually (mostly in judicial custody, largely due to illness; police custody deaths raise allegations of torture). Causes: (a) torture and custodial violence — blunt force injuries (beating with lathis, belts — 'tramline/railway track' bruises; falanga/falaka — beating the soles of feet; telefono — slapping both ears simultaneously causing tympanic rupture; roller — rolling a heavy log over thighs causing crush injuries and rhabdomyolysis with acute renal failure; suspension — 'Palestinian hanging', 'parrot perch' — brachial plexus injuries; submarino — head submersion in water; electric shocks — electrical burns at genitals, ear lobes, nipples; burns with cigarettes; sexual torture; nails pulled out; stress positions; sleep and food deprivation), rhabdomyolysis, multiple injuries, head injury, ruptured viscera; (b) suicide — hanging (with clothing, blankets, belts in lock-ups — must be distinguished from homicidal hanging/strangulation staged as suicide), self-harm; (c) natural disease — cardiovascular disease, tuberculosis, HIV, alcohol withdrawal (delirium tremens), drug withdrawal, diabetes, inadequate medical care and neglect; (d) restraint-related deaths — positional asphyxia (prone restraint, hog-tying), neck holds (chokehold — carotid compression), excited delirium; (e) firearm injuries ('encounter' killings, police firing); (f) poisoning (smuggled drugs, alcohol); (g) accidents, assault by other inmates. Procedure (legal requirements): (1) Magistrate's/judicial inquiry — under S. 196 BNSS (S. 176 CrPC), when a person dies or disappears or rape is alleged while in custody of police or in any other custody authorised by the magistrate or court, in addition to police investigation, an inquiry shall be held by the Judicial Magistrate or Metropolitan Magistrate within whose local jurisdiction the offence has been committed (S. 196(2) BNSS; S. 176(1A) CrPC inserted 2005); the magistrate shall, within 24 hours of the death, forward the body for examination to the nearest civil surgeon or qualified medical person; the relatives of the deceased are informed and allowed to be present at the inquiry; (2) NHRC guidelines (1993, 1995, 1997, 2010): all cases of custodial death (police and judicial custody) must be reported to the National Human Rights Commission by the District Magistrate/SP within 24 hours of occurrence; failure is presumed as an attempt to suppress the facts; post-mortem examination should be video-filmed and the cassette/CD with the PM report, inquest report and magisterial inquiry report sent to NHRC (within 2 months); NHRC model autopsy form (with detailed documentation of external injuries, photographs, body chart, torture signs, internal examination, histopathology and chemical analysis); autopsy preferably by a board of two or more doctors, at least one with forensic expertise, conducted at the earliest (and in daylight); relatives informed and may have their doctor present; (3) post-mortem considerations — detailed external examination of the entire body including soles, palms, genitals, back, scalp (shaved if needed), ears (tympanic membranes), nails; incisions over areas of suspected deep bruising (back, buttocks, thighs, soles — subcutaneous dissection — 'peel off' technique to reveal deep bruises not visible on skin, especially in dark-skinned persons and with blunt broad weapons); look for ligature marks, signs of restraint (handcuff marks), electrical marks (histology), burns, healing injuries of different ages (repeated torture), fractures (X-rays — whole-body), rhabdomyolysis (myoglobinuric renal failure — kidney histology, CPK), signs of asphyxia, sexual assault, poisoning (viscera); distinguish self-inflicted from inflicted injuries; injuries before arrest documented in arrest memo/medical examination at arrest; (4) D.K. Basu v. State of West Bengal (Supreme Court, 1997) — guidelines to prevent custodial violence: police personnel carrying out arrest must bear accurate, visible identification; memo of arrest attested by a witness and countersigned by arrestee; inform a friend/relative; inform arrestee of right; entry in diary; arrestee examined at the time of arrest for major and minor injuries — inspection memo signed by arrestee and police; medical examination by a trained doctor every 48 hours during detention; copies to magistrate; allowed to meet lawyer during interrogation; police control room information; now incorporated in BNSS (S. 35–37, S. 53 — medical examination of arrested person by medical officer immediately after arrest; copy to arrested person); (5) Prakash Singh (2006) police reforms; UN Convention against Torture (India signed 1997, not ratified); Protection of Human Rights Act 1993; Paramvir Singh Saini v. Baljit Singh (2020) — CCTV cameras in all police stations and central agencies with 18-month recordings; (6) the doctor's duty — impartial, thorough examination; documentation of torture (Istanbul Protocol, 1999 — Manual on Effective Investigation and Documentation of Torture); refusal to participate in torture (WMA Declaration of Tokyo, 1975); reporting injuries observed in prisoners; maintaining confidentiality while ensuring justice.",
[["Causes of sudden natural death",[["System","Common causes"],["Cardiovascular (most common)","Coronary atherosclerosis/MI, arrhythmia, HCM (young athletes), myocarditis, aortic stenosis, aortic aneurysm/dissection, pulmonary embolism, channelopathies"],["Respiratory","Massive haemoptysis (TB), asthma, pneumonia, laryngeal oedema, tension pneumothorax"],["CNS","SAH (berry aneurysm), hypertensive ICH, epilepsy (SUDEP), meningitis, colloid cyst"],["GIT","Haematemesis, perforated ulcer, acute pancreatitis, ruptured spleen"],["Genitourinary","Ruptured ectopic, eclampsia, amniotic fluid embolism"],["Others","Anaphylaxis, DKA/hypoglycaemia, adrenal haemorrhage, SIDS"]]],
["Medicolegal issues in sudden death",["Exclude poisoning/violence — full autopsy + toxicology","Trivial injury/stress precipitating death (eggshell skull rule)","Insurance, workmen's compensation","Deaths in custody, during sex, procedures","Negative autopsy; molecular autopsy for channelopathies"]],
["Custodial death — procedure",[["Requirement","Details"],["Inquiry","Judicial magistrate — S. 196(2) BNSS (176(1A) CrPC)"],["NHRC intimation","Within 24 h"],["Autopsy","Board of ≥ 2 doctors; video-filmed; NHRC model form"],["Reports to NHRC","Video, PM, inquest, magisterial inquiry"],["Relatives","Informed; may be present"]]],
["Signs of torture",["Tramline bruises, deep bruises (subcutaneous dissection)","Falanga (soles), telefono (tympanic rupture)","Roller injuries → rhabdomyolysis, renal failure","Suspension injuries, handcuff marks","Electrical burns (genitals, ears), cigarette burns","Injuries of different ages"]],
["D.K. Basu guidelines (1997)",["Identification of arresting police","Arrest memo, witness, inform relative","Inspection memo of injuries at arrest","Medical examination every 48 h","Access to lawyer","Copies to magistrate; CCTV in police stations (2020)"]]],
{c:"Sudden natural deaths, mostly cardiovascular (especially coronary artery disease), must be fully autopsied to exclude violence or poisoning and resolve trauma-versus-disease questions, while custodial deaths require a judicial magistrate's inquiry, intimation to NHRC within 24 hours and a videographed board autopsy searching meticulously for signs of torture, in line with D.K. Basu safeguards."});

Z("fmt4",8,"f",["Exhumation is the lawful digging out (disinterment) of a body already buried, for medicolegal examination, ordered only by a magistrate (S. 196 BNSS / 176(3) CrPC); in India there is no time limit; it is done to establish identity, cause of death (especially metallic poisoning), or to re-examine when the first autopsy is disputed, with soil samples collected from around the body for comparison.","Exhumation reopens a closed chapter — when new suspicion arises after burial, the law permits the body to be brought back for questioning.",["Indications — criminal: suspected homicide/poisoning (arsenic, antimony, lead, mercury, thallium persist), criminal abortion, death due to negligence, disputed identity, second autopsy when first is incomplete/disputed; civil: insurance, workmen's compensation, survivorship, inheritance, identity, professional negligence.","Legal: only a magistrate (DM/SDM/Executive or Judicial Magistrate) can order (S. 196(3) BNSS); no time limit in India (several countries have limits — e.g., France 10 years, Germany 30 years); NHRC requires in custodial deaths if buried without PM.","Procedure: written order; site identified by relatives/cemetery records; presence of magistrate, doctor, police, relatives; early morning/daylight; protective clothing; earth removed carefully; soil samples ~500 g from above, below and both sides of the body + control soil from distance; coffin, shroud, fluid collected; body identified; examination at site or mortuary; avoid disinfectants on body; record changes.","Value: depends on time and conditions — injuries to bones, fractures, bullets, metallic poisons, teeth and bones for identity, age, sex; adipocere/mummification may preserve injuries; organic poisons usually lost; Casper's dictum — burial slows decomposition 8×."],"Exhumation: magistrate's order only; no time limit in India; collect soil samples (metallic poisons)."],
"Exhumation is the lawful digging out of a buried body from the grave for the purpose of medicolegal examination (identification or determination of cause of death); cremated bodies cannot be exhumed (only bones/ashes may be examined — limited value), so medicolegal autopsy before disposal is important. Legal aspects: in India, exhumation can be ordered only by a magistrate — District Magistrate, Sub-divisional Magistrate or any Executive Magistrate empowered (S. 196(3) BNSS, 2023, corresponding to S. 176(3) CrPC: 'where the magistrate considers it expedient to make an examination of the dead body of any person who has been already interred, in order to discover the cause of his death, the magistrate may cause the body to be disinterred and examined'); police cannot order exhumation; there is no time limit for exhumation in India (in other countries: France — 10 years, Germany — 30 years, Scotland — 20 years, some none); exhumation of a body for religious or family reasons (transfer to another grave) requires permission from local authorities; in custodial deaths or deaths of women within 7 years of marriage where the body was buried without proper investigation, exhumation is commonly ordered. Indications: (A) criminal cases — (1) suspected homicide discovered after burial (new evidence, complaints from relatives, confessions), (2) suspected poisoning — particularly with metallic (inorganic) poisons like arsenic, antimony, lead, mercury, thallium, copper, which remain in the tissues, hair, nails and bones for years and also retard decomposition (arsenic-preserved bodies), (3) criminal abortion, (4) death due to alleged medical negligence (operation, anaesthesia), (5) disputed identity (wrong body buried, substitution), (6) second autopsy when the first autopsy was incomplete, its findings disputed, or new questions arise (e.g., in custodial death, dowry death), (7) bodies buried secretly by murderers (clandestine burials) — strictly speaking 'recovery of buried remains' rather than exhumation from a legal grave, but similar procedure; (B) civil cases — (1) accidental death insurance claims, (2) workmen's compensation (occupational disease or accident), (3) survivorship (who died first), (4) disputed inheritance and identity (DNA from bones/teeth for paternity), (5) professional negligence claims, (6) legitimacy. Procedure: (1) written order of the magistrate to the medical officer (forensic expert) specifying the purpose; (2) the exhumation is carried out in the presence of the magistrate (or his representative), the medical officer, police officer, and identifying persons (relatives, cemetery/graveyard keeper who identifies the grave from records); photographer/videographer; (3) timing — preferably early in the morning in daylight (sufficient time and less heat/odour), avoiding public crowds; (4) precautions — the grave is first identified and photographed with its marker; workers wear protective clothing, gloves and masks (risk of infections, toxic gases — H₂S, methane, ammonia in vaults); no smoking (inflammable gases); disinfectant (lime/bleaching powder) may be sprinkled on the surroundings but not on the body or coffin (to avoid contaminating samples); (5) excavation — the earth is removed carefully layer by layer without damaging the coffin or body; (6) soil samples — in suspected poisoning, about 500 g of earth is collected each from above the body/coffin, beneath, and from both sides, along with a control sample of soil (about 500 g) from a distant part of the cemetery (at the same depth) where no body is buried — to exclude the possibility that the poison (e.g., arsenic present naturally in soil or derived from shrouds, flowers, fertilisers, coffin paints/preservatives) diffused into the body from the soil; water accumulated in the grave and the shroud/coffin lining are also collected; (7) the coffin is opened and the body is identified by relatives (clothing, shroud, coffin, jewellery, features) and recorded; (8) the body is examined at the site (if conditions permit) or transported in a sealed container to the mortuary for autopsy; the degree of decomposition, adipocere, mummification, presence of insects noted; complete autopsy performed; X-rays for fractures, bullets; samples collected — viscera (stomach, intestines, liver, kidneys), hair (whole length with roots — segmental analysis for chronic arsenic poisoning), nails, bones (long bones), teeth, skin, muscle, maggots/pupae (entomotoxicology), samples for DNA (teeth, bones); (9) after examination the body is re-buried; (10) the report includes the conditions of the grave, findings, and opinion with limitations due to decomposition. Medicolegal value (what can be determined): the value diminishes with time and depends on depth of burial, type of soil (dry sandy soil preserves; wet clay soils with water promote adipocere), coffin, climate and embalming; even after long periods, the following may be established: (a) identity — sex, age, stature, race from skeleton; teeth and dental work; old fractures, implants; DNA from bones/teeth; (b) injuries to bones — fractures (skull, hyoid in strangulation, ribs), cut marks on bones, firearm injuries (bullets and pellets recovered; entry and exit holes in skull bones); (c) metallic poisons — arsenic, antimony, lead, mercury, thallium can be detected in hair, nails, bones even after many years (e.g., Napoleon's hair arsenic studies); organic poisons (alkaloids, OP compounds, cyanide) are generally lost or difficult to interpret, though some (strychnine, barbiturates) are stable; alcohol may be produced by putrefaction; (d) soft-tissue findings if adipocere or mummification has preserved the body (wounds, ligature marks); (e) pregnancy (fetal bones), criminal abortion (instruments, injury), etc. Limitations: decomposition destroying soft tissue evidence and producing artefacts (post-mortem fractures due to weight of earth, rodents), contamination from soil, embalming fluids (formaldehyde, methanol, arsenic formerly), and difficulty in interpretation; Casper's dictum indicates that a body buried in soil decomposes about eight times slower than in air, so exhumation even after weeks may give useful findings.",
[["Indications",[["Criminal","Civil"],["Suspected homicide after burial","Accident insurance claims"],["Poisoning (metallic poisons)","Workmen's compensation"],["Criminal abortion","Survivorship, inheritance"],["Medical negligence","Disputed identity, paternity (DNA)"],["Disputed identity; second autopsy","Professional negligence"]]],
["Procedure",["Magistrate's written order (S. 196(3) BNSS)","Presence: magistrate, doctor, police, relatives, grave keeper","Daylight, early morning; protective gear","Identify and photograph grave","Remove earth carefully","Soil ~500 g above, below, both sides + control sample","Collect coffin lining, shroud, fluid","Identify body; autopsy; X-rays","Samples: viscera, hair, nails, bones, teeth","Re-burial; report with limitations"]],
["What can be found after long burial",[["Finding","Persistence"],["Identity (skeleton, teeth, DNA)","Years"],["Fractures, cut marks, bullets","Years"],["Arsenic, antimony, lead, mercury, thallium","Years (hair, nails, bones)"],["Organic poisons","Usually lost"],["Soft-tissue injuries","If adipocere/mummification"]]],
["Legal points",["Only magistrate can order (not police)","No time limit in India","Cremated bodies cannot be exhumed","Common in dowry and custodial deaths"]]],
{c:"Exhumation is the magistrate-ordered (S. 196(3) BNSS), time-unlimited disinterment of a buried body for medicolegal examination in criminal or civil disputes, conducted with formal identification, protective measures and collection of control soil samples, and it can still reveal identity, bony injuries, projectiles and metallic poisons long after burial."});
